WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:35.930
Hello, my name is Zisl Slepovitch

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:38.701
and I'm a musician-in-residence at Yale

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:41.079
University's Fortunoff Video Archive

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:42.730
for Holocaust Testimonies.

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:45.789
This video series is
designed as a companion

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:47.950
to our album, Where Is Our Homeland,

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.469
which is the first volume in the Archive's

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:51.989
ongoing effort to present songs

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:54.555
and poems performed by survivors in

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:57.699
testimonies given to the Fortunoff Archive

00:00:57.880 --> 00:00:59.670
over the last 40 years.

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:02.049
You can learn more about the project on the

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:05.900
Archives website, songsfromtestimonies.org.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:08.049
Where Is Our Homeland?

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:09.863
Songs from Testimonies Volume One was

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:12.660
recorded in 2019 by

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:15.199
the Zisl Slepovitch Ensemble featuring

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.944
renowned singer Sasha Lurje,

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:19.514
Craig Judelman on the violin, Joshua

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.999
Camp on the accordion,
keyboards and guitar,

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:26.089
Dmitry Ishenko on the bass, and myself

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:27.090
on woodwinds.

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:29.815
The songs in this
collection differ vastly in

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:31.933
terms of style, social and historical

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:34.799
background, language, and many other

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:37.259
aspects, but collectively they

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.024
create a multidimensional picture of

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:42.660
Jewish life before and during World War II.

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:47.930
This song that we're going to talk about

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:50.119
and listen to today

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:53.740
was the song of
the Płaszów inmates.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:58.010
It was sung, or

00:01:58.020 --> 00:01:59.930
rather recited as a poem

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:01.730
by Ruth C.,

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:04.999
a native of Kraków, Poland,

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:07.999
where she was born in 1920.

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:10.849
During the Holocaus, Ruth

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:13.489
survived several labor camps

00:02:13.490 --> 00:02:15.050
and concentration camps

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:17.687
in both her native country and in

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:19.440
Germany.

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:22.009
The inmates wrote

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:24.709
this poem. They were quietly passing

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:27.100
it on to

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:29.570
each other as a means of hope

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:32.749
for survival, as a hope

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:34.579
to escape from

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:37.390
the deadly cemetery gate

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:39.019
as the song goes.

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:48.820
I can tell you the whole song in Polish

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:50.940
Every morning at five,

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:52.860
The trumpet wakes us up.

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:54.620
With its familiar call

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:56.900
To the Appelplatz.

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:59.340
We stand straight in rows,

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:00.860
Looking into the distance,

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:02.860
Looking to escape

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:04.660
This cemetery gate.

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:07.820
Dark night hovers over the roofs,

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:10.460
The city is sound asleep.

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:12.540
Only we — just one more step,

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:14.740
And the heart will die in pain.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:16.340
Something like this,

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:18.540
but perhaps the last stanza

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:20.540
isn't completely correct,

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:23.500
but this is the song.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:44.460
Every morning at five,

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:47.940
The trumpet wakes us up.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:51.860
With its familiar call

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:55.340
To the Appelplatz.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:59.180
We stand straight in rows,

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:03.100
Looking into the distance,

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:06.780
Looking to escape

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:10.420
This cemetery gate.

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:14.300
Looking to escape

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:17.900
This cemetery gate.

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:33.740
Dark night hovers over the roofs,

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:38.100
The city is sound asleep.

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:42.940
Only we — just one more step,

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:48.060
And the heart will die in pain.

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:52.540
Only we — just one more step,

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:57.740
And the heart will die in pain.

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:12.460
Only we — just one more step,

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:17.900
And the heart will die in pain.

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:22.060
Only we — just one more step,

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:33.420
And the heart will die in pain.

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:46.860
The lyrics of this hymn were led by a

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:49.500
survivor of the Płaszów concentration

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:51.790
camp named Ruth Cohen,

00:05:52.390 --> 00:05:54.279
who believes it was written by one of her

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:55.280
fellow inmates.

00:05:56.260 --> 00:05:58.149
It speaks to the terrible feeling that

00:05:58.150 --> 00:05:59.979
prisoners felt at roll call,

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:02.352
appelplatz, every morning at 5:00 a.m.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:05.139
in the camp, when the prisoners lined

00:06:05.140 --> 00:06:07.689
up and felt as if they are one step

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:08.690
away from death.

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:12.460
The notorious camp
commander, Amon Göth,

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:15.020
who was portrayed by the actor Ralph Finnes

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:17.425
in the film Schindler's
List, would sometimes

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:19.837
randomly shoot the prisoners at a whim

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:21.640
during his roll calls.

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:25.120
Płaszów represented more than the deaths

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:26.650
of individuals.

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:29.729
Płaszów represented the death of the Kraków

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:30.730
Jewish community.

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:33.459
So by thinking about the Kraków Jewish

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:35.799
community before the Holocaust, we

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:37.858
can better appreciate the immensity of the

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:38.930
loss.

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:42.059
Kraków had been a major religious center.

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:44.399
Throughout the early modern period, it was

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:47.249
home to several leading rabbinical figures

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:49.900
such as Rabbi Moses Isserles,

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:53.180
Mordechai Jaffe, Joel Sirkes.

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:55.949
The visitor to Kraków today can still

00:06:55.950 --> 00:06:58.410
see the Old Synagogue's

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:01.349
testament to its former glory, the

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:03.140
Old Jewish Cemetery.

00:07:03.430 --> 00:07:06.380
But by the interwar period, Kraków Jewry

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:08.070
had become much more diverse.

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:11.260
Kraków was home to theaters, cabarets,

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:12.820
literary cafes.

00:07:13.630 --> 00:07:16.140
The great folk songwriter Mordechai

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:18.830
Gebirtig, who lived in Kraków,

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:21.799
wrote the song Es brent,
Our Town Is Burning,

00:07:22.370 --> 00:07:24.300
a defiant song calling on

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:26.899
Jews to rise up and defend

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:29.089
themselves during the pogroms in the late

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:30.140
1930s.

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:33.959
Kraków is also home to many Hasidic Jews,

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:36.410
followers of the great Bobover Rebbe

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:38.260
and the Belzer Rebbe.

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:41.210
And it was home to a teacher seminary

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:43.900
to train teachers for Orthodox

00:07:44.060 --> 00:07:46.039
and Hasidic girls and women.

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:50.130
Kraków was also a major Zionist center,

00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:53.300
and this was the affiliation of Ruth Cohen

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:54.460
and her family.

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:56.790
The great rabbi, Ozjasz Thon,

00:07:57.970 --> 00:08:00.130
preached in the temple synagogue

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:01.913
and was a fervent Zionist.

00:08:02.890 --> 00:08:05.410
The Sejm, or parliament deputy,

00:08:06.010 --> 00:08:08.469
Apolinary Hartglas, was also

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:09.760
a Zionist and lived in Kraków.

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:13.043
In the wake of World War I,

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:16.109
during the pogroms that occurred in Kraków,

00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:18.637
the Zionists were absolutely crucial to the

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:20.348
formation of Jewish self-defense

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:22.090
units.

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:25.539
And after this, they formed a

00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:28.340
major newspaper, Nowy Dziennik,

00:08:28.460 --> 00:08:30.469
which had a circulation of around fifteen

00:08:30.470 --> 00:08:31.470
thousand.

00:08:32.530 --> 00:08:34.540
Zionists held about half the seats

00:08:34.870 --> 00:08:37.300
and the Kehila or the Jewish communal

00:08:37.389 --> 00:08:38.389
self-government.

00:08:39.129 --> 00:08:41.619
The other half was held

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:44.229
by Hasidic and assimilationists Jews.

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:47.257
There was also a very popular Zionist youth

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:49.229
group named Akiba, which would

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:52.319
perform a major role in

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:54.629
self-defense during the Holocaust.

00:08:56.190 --> 00:08:59.140
Ruth Cohen describes
her upbringing in Kraków

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:02.229
as occurring between two worlds,

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:03.950
one Polish, one Hebrew.

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:07.199
Ruth attended the Zionist

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:10.170
gymnasium where they would learn,

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:12.738
study in Polish half the day and

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:15.170
in Hebrew the other half of the day.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:19.460
She thought of herself as really

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:21.599
a Zionist, but her family

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:24.000
did not have any thoughts of immigrating

00:09:24.510 --> 00:09:25.599
to Palestine.

00:09:26.620 --> 00:09:28.839
They tried to escape
when the war broke out,

00:09:29.530 --> 00:09:30.809
but it was just too late.

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:35.359
First, they lived in the Kraków ghetto

00:09:36.110 --> 00:09:38.719
and then on March 13th,

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:41.329
they were deported,
along with most of Kraków

00:09:41.330 --> 00:09:43.980
Jews to the Płaszów concentration camp.

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:46.159
By this time, there were about twenty

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:48.530
thousand Jews were prisoners there.

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:51.140
And the camp was run by the notorious Amon

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:54.260
Göth, the sadistic SS Kommandant

00:09:54.891 --> 00:09:57.290
Oskar Schindler's
enamel factory was located

00:09:57.291 --> 00:09:58.400
adjacent to the camp.

00:09:59.331 --> 00:10:01.910
Göth and the other guards shot thousands

00:10:01.911 --> 00:10:04.190
of Jews and deported thousands

00:10:04.191 --> 00:10:07.580
more to their deaths in Auschwitz.

00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:10.460
According to Ruth, everyone was afraid

00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:12.900
all the time, but especially

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:16.460
terrified at 5:00 a.m.

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:18.589
in the morning, when they would line up

00:10:18.590 --> 00:10:20.900
for roll call, Appelplatz.

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:24.380
The song, the Hymn of Płaszów ,

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:28.169
reads as follows: Every morning at 5:00,

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:31.019
the trumpet wakes up with its familiar call

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:32.500
to the Appelplatz.

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:35.069
We stand in straight rows.

00:10:35.490 --> 00:10:37.739
If a hand were to signal in the distance,

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:40.259
we would all escape as soon as possible

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:42.240
from the cemetery gate.

00:10:43.570 --> 00:10:45.628
Dark night hovers over the roofs, the city

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:48.620
is sound asleep, only we just one

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:51.110
more step, and the heart will

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:52.120
die in pain.

00:10:53.410 --> 00:10:55.899
It's especially poignant
to compare this song

00:10:56.200 --> 00:10:58.420
to the pre-war song by Gebirtig,

00:10:58.580 --> 00:11:00.379
Es brent, Our Town is Burning,

00:11:00.980 --> 00:11:02.780
which urged the Jews to rise

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:05.540
up against their persecutors and

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:06.820
fight.

00:11:06.980 --> 00:11:09.420
And certainly during the Holocaust,

00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:11.380
members of youth group Akiba

00:11:12.250 --> 00:11:13.419
did just that.

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:17.620
However, a more

00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:20.429
representative feeling is probably captured

00:11:20.430 --> 00:11:22.679
in this song, a feeling of despair,

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:25.460
a feeling of terror, a longing

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:28.069
for escape that rarely occurred.

